[Music]  time now for our morning rounds this  week we focus on one topic epileptic  seizures according to the Epilepsy  Foundation around 48 of every 100,000  people will develop epilepsy the  condition tends to be more common among  young children and older people and the  seizures can be debilitating but there  is new hope thanks to advancements in  science and technology we're joined by  CBS News chief medical correspondent dr.  Jon LaPook who took a closer look at one  of these developments good morning good  morning anti-seizure medication alone  doesn't work well enough in about one of  three people with epilepsy and for the  past two years  I followed the journey of an  extraordinary family who turned to a  novel treatment eleven-year-old Isabella  Renaldo is a medical pioneer at 18  months she had her first epileptic  seizure at its worst there were five or  six a day she's had them so severe where  they literally knock her off her feet  mom Regina it's heartbreaking it's sad  you know you don't want to see your  child confused and and frightened  medications were failing to control the  epilepsy when two years ago Isabella  became the youngest person implanted  with a kind of defibrillator for the  brain called a neuro pace it was no  precedent for this in somebody her age  what was that like for you that was  scary and and we said alright we know  we're not gonna rush into it and then we  had a really bad day and we were back in  the ER and we said we gotta do this I  can see the seizures building up  building up building up building up dr.  Steven Wolfe specializes in pediatric  epilepsy at Mount Sinai Medical Center  and we found that we were able to stop  her seizures by stimulating it with  electricity so basically we were  fighting fire with fire the device  learns to recognize abnormal brainwave  patterns that left alone would trigger a  seizure but by delivering a small shock  within milliseconds it normalizes brain  activity and prevents the seizure before  surgery doctors make a map of the brain  to pinpoint exactly where seizures occur  it looks like she's about to have a  seizure right now for Isabella  it's where hand and arm movements are  controlled during surgery electrodes are  placed at those sites one month after  surgery the device is switched on you  are now officially stimulating your  brain  we followed Isabella over the next two  years as she was carefully monitored and  the seizures became less frequent we got  an update from the ronaldo's this past  fall  we've been seizure free for six months  and we actually started to wean one of  her medications before she was more  subdued tired now she is more alert  she's more energetic she's more eager to  try new things you can do karate right I  have to learn to let go a little bit  more because I'm just so used to being  all over her waiting for something to  happen and you know sometimes I'll jump  in and like I'm 11 I can do it myself  how was mom feeling moms feeling proud  how's isabella feeling she just turned  12 there sent 12 in March I spoke to her  mom yesterday and you know when I last  spoke to her mom she said you know she's  becoming a little sassy when I spoke to  her yesterday I said if she's still  sassy she said yeah she's still sassy  but you know what she's earned it yes  good we like sass you know I think  people in America might be surprised  just how many people actually suffer  from seizures do you think this really  is a game changer well I think it's one  piece of the puzzle you have about  two-thirds of people whose seizures are  adequately controlled with medication  but then for the other third it can be a  real problem and devastating especially  if you're a kid you're trying to fit in  with your schoolmates and you're trying  to have normal development so for those  other third there there's other things  there's a ketogenic diet there's  something called the vagus nerve  stimulation there's new medications that  are 